{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -C 3 'arithmetic builtins|float64|carrier operands|documented|limitations' docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md | sed -n '1,210p'\"","aggregated_output":"205-\n206-Jing's four names use tag 27 followed by exactly `[name-string, payload]`:\n207-`dao.jing/list` takes an array, `dao.jing/keyword` and `dao.jing/symbol` take\n208:the two-component arrays above, and `dao.jing/float64` takes an eight-byte\n209-byte string. Tag 27's object meaning is verified against the\n210-[IANA registry](https://www.iana.org/assignments/cbor-tags/cbor-tags.xhtml);\n211-the array-payload grammar and public mechanisms are verified against\n--\n335-for numbers. In `dao.space.query`, the ordering builtins (`< > <= >= min\n336-max`) route through the portable compare above; `=` and `not=` bind to\n337-`dao.jing.cbor/content=` per the ruling above, not host `=`. The\n338:arithmetic builtins\n339-(`+ - * / quot rem mod inc dec abs`) remain host-native and reject carrier\n340-operands loudly rather than computing garbage — queries that combine\n341:arithmetic builtins with floating, decimal, or rational datom values are\n342-therefore not portable across hosts until arithmetic itself is made\n343-portable, a recorded loud limitation consistent with VM arithmetic\n344-remaining outside this migration. Where host-native dispatch cannot\n--\n357-whichever operand a fold happens to see last, which depends on argument\n358-order and is not a rule (`(min 1 1.0)` and `(min 1.0 1)` disagree on the\n359-JVM today). The portable versions instead: (1) if exactly one operand is\n360:`float64`, return the other one — integer, decimal, and rational are all\n361:finite (exact) precision, and float64 is the one inexact IEEE-754 kind, so\n362-it always loses a tie to an exact operand; (2) if both operands are\n363:`float64`, or both are exact but distinct (different kind, or the same\n364-kind at a different scale or sign), return whichever operand has the\n365-shorter canonical CBOR encoding — the more compact representation, for\n366-example decimal `1.0` (exponent `-1`, mantissa `10`, a one-byte shortest\n--\n372-three cases are independent of argument order and of which host runs them:\n373-`(min 1 1.0)` and `(min 1.0 1)` both return `1`.\n374-\n375:- Provide a portable `float64` constructor/carrier. JavaScript callers use\n376-  it for integral floating-point values such as `1.0`; ordinary integral\n377-  JavaScript numbers remain integers, except native negative zero, which\n378-  is floating-point content so its sign survives. Decoding floating-point content on\n379-  JavaScript returns the carrier so re-encoding cannot lose its kind.\n380-- Encode floating-point content on every host as tag 27,\n381:  `dao.jing/float64`, with eight big-endian IEEE-754 bytes. This avoids\n382-  Boring's native-number differences between JVM and JavaScript, verified\n383-  against its compatibility policy above: integral JS numbers encode as\n384-  integers while JVM `1.0` remains a float under `:canonical`.\n385:  Widen native float32 values to float64, preserve signed zero and\n386-  infinities, and canonicalize NaNs to quiet NaN bits `0x7ff8000000000000`.\n387-  **The Jing profile emits no native CBOR floating-point whatsoever**;\n388-  Boring's canonical float narrowing and NaN encoding never govern Jing\n389:  floating-point bytes. The carrier is Jing-owned; upstream has no float64\n390-  wrapper (verified against Boring `data.cljc`, 2026-09-16).\n391-- Use CBOR integers and bignum tags 2/3 for exact integers. Never convert\n392-  large integers through a floating-point intermediate. Reject unsafe\n393-  integral JavaScript Number inputs; callers must supply BigInt for exact\n394:  integers or the float64 carrier for floating-point content.\n395-- Use decimal-fraction tag 4 with `[exponent mantissa]`; preserve decimal\n396-  scale. Use rational tag 30 with a reduced numerator and positive,\n397-  nonzero denominator. Retain rational kind even when the denominator is 1.\n--\n546-\n547-Update the main Jing design, backend documentation, and downstream canonical\n548-encoding references to describe the shared byte-store boundary and remove\n549:the superseded transitional-printer limitations. Name exactly which\n550-`dao.jing.md` open items this plan retires — the canonical-encoding item\n551-with its three residuals (symbol metadata not address-significant,\n552-pathological print collisions, ambient print-var leakage), byte-array\n--\n572-   `dao.jing.cbor` exports the portable `=`/`hash`/`compare`; the numeric\n573-   arm of `dao.space.index/compare-vals` (the EAVT/AEVT/AVET/VAET datom\n574-   comparators) and `dao.space.query`'s ordering builtins (`< > <= >= min\n575:   max`) route through them, with the arithmetic builtins left host-native\n576-   per *Numeric identity* — the deepest of this migration's changes outside\n577-   the `dao.jing*` namespaces, threading new runtime behavior into an\n578-   existing consumer's ordering logic. Per the owner ruling in *Numeric\n--\n610-  host's one-argument constructor parses slash text. All identifiers use\n611-  named frames; reject tag 39 even for ordinary names.\n612-- Frozen read/write fixtures for each of `dao.jing/list`,\n613:  `dao.jing/keyword`, `dao.jing/symbol`, and `dao.jing/float64`, including\n614-  malformed payload shapes; closed UnknownRecord conversion and effective\n615-  stringref/shapes/index disabling.\n616-- Byte-array content identity and mutation isolation before and after reads.\n617-- Integer versus integral float; signed zero; infinities and NaNs; integer\n618-  boundaries and bignums; decimal scale; reduced ratios and denominator 1.\n619-- Native float32 widening: a host float32 0.1 encodes the same eight bytes\n620:  as its exact float64 widening, distinct from float64 0.1; Node and Dart\n621-  reproduce the widened fixture, including signed-zero widening.\n622-- Node/Dart decoded numeric carriers in index-ordered datom positions\n623-  (`dao.space.index/compare-vals`, no string fallback) and\n624:  `dao.space.query` matched positions: native/carrier operands both ways,\n625-  mixed kinds, exact large values, ordering, signed zeros, infinities,\n626-  NaN, and preserved kind on re-encoding. Also: the ordering builtins\n627-  (`< > <= >= min max`) over decoded carriers; `=`/`not=` and Datalog\n628-  unification bind to `dao.jing.cbor/content=`, kind-strict per the owner\n629-  ruling in *Numeric identity*, so a collection value containing a carrier\n630-  unifies only with a value of the same kind, scale and sign; the\n631:  arithmetic builtins over carriers\n632-  reject loudly; and `[e a 1 t m]` versus `[e a 1.0 t m]` collapse to one\n633-  covered-index entry while keeping two distinct content addresses and not\n634-  unifying against each other in a query.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
