{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '175,230p;485,580p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '175,230p;485,580p'\"","aggregated_output":"   175\taddress contains different canonical bytes is an integrity failure, not an\n   176\toverwrite, and throws loudly. `nil` is a legal payload distinct from absence:\n   177\t`get` takes a caller-supplied not-found and returns stored `nil` as `nil`.\n   178\t\n   179\t### Canonical encoding\n   180\t\n   181\tContent addressing is meaningful only when equal supported values produce the\n   182\tsame bytes on every participating platform. Distinct values must address\n   183\tdistinctly, both across types (`42` and `\"42\"` do not collide) and within one\n   184\ttype (`{:a 1}` and `{:a 2}` do not). Minted addresses are readable EDN: the\n   185\tname never starts with a digit, and a key survives `pr-str` → `read-string`.\n   186\tThe canonical encoder is therefore part of the storage contract even though the\n   187\tmeaning of the encoded value is not.\n   188\t\n   189\tCanonicalization may understand representation-level structure such as maps,\n   190\tsets, numbers, strings, and byte arrays. It must not understand domain concepts\n   191\tsuch as datoms, index orders, manifests, or any notion of a root.\n   192\t\n   193\tThe encoding is the pinned, cross-platform canonical CBOR profile of\n   194\t[`dao.jing.cbor.md`](dao.jing.cbor.md), implemented by `dao.jing.cbor/encode`\n   195\tand `decode` and frozen by the `cbor-v1` fixture corpus:\n   196\t`dao.jing/canonical-bytes` is `dao.jing.cbor/encode`, and every address is\n   197\tthe selected registry digest of exactly those bytes on every host.\n   198\t\n   199\tThe contract, precisely: collection metadata (on maps, sets, vectors, lists,\n   200\tand seqs) and symbol metadata are address-significant, except\n   201\treader-position keys (`:line`, `:column`, `:end-line`, `:end-column`), which\n   202\tare stripped before hashing, and empty metadata, which is dropped rather than\n   203\ttreated as distinct from no metadata; a metadata map that itself carries\n   204\tmetadata is refused. Lists and seqs of equal content share one address;\n   205\tvectors, sets, and maps are each their own type and never collide with\n   206\tanother. Keywords and symbols are encoded from their namespace and name\n   207\tfields, so a symbol never collides with the number or string its text\n   208\tmimics. Byte arrays are addressed by content. Numeric kind, decimal scale,\n   209\tand float zero sign are address-significant; host integer width is not.\n   210\tAnything outside the supported domain (records, functions, host objects,\n   211\tcharacters, `#inst`, `#uuid`) is refused loudly rather than addressed.\n   212\t`materialize!`'s `:present` read-back is verified by hash and byte for byte\n   213\tagainst the proposed bytes, so a metadata-only mismatch is caught as a real\n   214\tcollision.\n   215\t\n   216\t## Multihash Content Addressing & Algorithm Registry\n   217\t\n   218\tDaoJing implements a closed, immutable algorithm registry providing\n   219\tmulti-algorithm content-addressing across JVM, Node/CLJS, and ClojureDart.\n   220\t\n   221\tSix core concepts are strictly distinguished:\n   222\t\n   223\t1. **Algorithm:** A registered cryptographic hash function identified by a\n   224\t   keyword (`:blake3` or `:sha256`). The registry is closed and immutable:\n   225\t   - `:blake3`: default minting algorithm; produces 32-byte (256-bit) digests.\n   226\t   - `:sha256`: permanent first-class selectable algorithm; produces 32-byte\n   227\t     digests.\n   228\t2. **Digest:** The lowercase hexadecimal representation of the raw digest\n   229\t   bytes computed over `canonical-bytes(payload)`.\n   230\t3. **Address:** The namespace-qualified keyword\n   485\twith no atoms, globals, registration, or discovery. The source stream never\n   486\tenters an address or a stored value.\n   487\t\n   488\t**Content addressing is implemented via the closed multihash registry.**\n   489\t`content-hash` digests canonical bytes under the selected algorithm (:blake3\n   490\tdefault, :sha256 selectable); `segment-key` mints\n   491\t`:segment/<algorithm>-<digest>` addresses; `segment-address?` is the strict\n   492\taddress test the backend layer enforces; `segment-matches?` (over a value)\n   493\tand `segment-bytes-match?` (over canonical bytes) are the total\n   494\taddress-directed verification predicates. As recorded in *Canonical\n   495\tencoding*, the encoder is the pinned canonical CBOR profile.\n   496\t\n   497\t**Backends are byte stores.** A handle is `{:put-bytes-fn f, :get-bytes-fn\n   498\tg, :close-fn c}`: put takes an address and canonical payload bytes and\n   499\tanswers `:inserted` or `:present`; get takes an address and a caller-supplied\n   500\tnot-found sentinel and answers bytes or that sentinel. `materialize!` encodes\n   501\ta payload exactly once, derives the address from those bytes, and hands the\n   502\tsame bytes to the backend; on `:present` it reads the bytes back and\n   503\tverifies them by hash and byte for byte. `get` hash-verifies the backend's\n   504\tbytes and decodes them. `dao.jing.remote` and `dao.jing.dht` carry the bytes\n   505\tas padded standard-alphabet Base64 text inside their existing Transit and\n   506\tdatagram envelopes, and run the one ingress canonicality check (strict\n   507\tBase64, digest, canonical decode) before any received bytes are stored or\n   508\tserved.\n   509\t\n   510\t## Open items and current limitations\n   511\t\n   512\t- **Retired by the canonical CBOR codec (2026-09-24).** The transitional\n   513\t  order-normalized printer, its three residuals (symbol metadata not\n   514\t  address-significant, pathological print collisions, ambient print-var\n   515\t  leakage), byte arrays hashed by identity, and the \"backends must fail\n   516\t  closed on metadata they cannot carry\" item are closed:\n   517\t  `dao.jing/canonical-bytes` is `dao.jing.cbor/encode`, every backend is a\n   518\t  byte store that carries the canonical bytes verbatim, and the file\n   519\t  backend's frame is the CBOR `[digest payload-bytes]` record. The\n   520\t  `pr-str`/EDN round-trip validator the file backend once needed is gone.\n   521\t  What remains open from that family is below.\n   522\t- **ClojureDart's `list` mints metadata.** On ClojureDart, `(list ...)` and\n   523\t  `(apply list ...)` return a list carrying `cljd.core`'s own reader metadata\n   524\t  (`{:line ... :column ... :end-line ... :end-column ... :tag\n   525\t  PersistentList}`), and vector literals can carry a Dart `Type` under\n   526\t  `:tag`. The codec strips reader positions but refuses a `Type`, so a\n   527\t  Dart producer must clear constructor metadata itself\n   528\t  (`(with-meta (apply list xs) nil)`) before handing a payload to\n   529\t  `dao.jing`; decoded lists carry none. This obligation carries forward\n   530\t  unchanged from `dao.jing.cbor.md`.\n   531\t- **The intake transport's portable domain is narrower than Jing's.** The\n   532\t  `dao.stream` Transit codec carries no metadata, byte strings, or rich\n   533\t  numerics, so such payloads reach Jing only through direct `materialize!`\n   534\t  calls; the intake fail-closed rule carries forward unchanged.\n   535\t- **Decoded numeric carriers on JavaScript.** Floating-point, decimal, and\n   536\t  rational content decode to Jing's portable carriers on JavaScript, never\n   537\t  to a bare number; a consumer that reads values back from a store and\n   538\t  pattern-matches on host number types (today, `yin.vm.debruijn`'s slot\n   539\t  type check) must accept the carriers, per `dao.jing.cbor.md`'s numeric\n   540\t  identity section.\n   541\t- **Durable observer checkpoints / long-running runner.** `observer-state`\n   542\t  and `observe-step!` are single-step and in-process. The checkpoint records,\n   543\t  per member, the stream coordinate plus the transport-minted cursor; the\n   544\t  coordinate is operational and never enters an address (serializability TBD in\n   545\t  the contract). A runner that drives the loop over time remains to be built.\n   546\t- **Explicit materialization acknowledgement.** A publisher observes only\n   547\t  that its payloads were appended to an intake stream. The mechanism by which\n   548\t  it observes that those payloads have been materialized must be expressed\n   549\t  explicitly, potentially as a response stream.\n   550\t- **The content write path as an effect stream.** A synchronous handle that\n   551\t  accepts a write and blocks until durable keeps the storage coupling. The\n   552\t  content write path as an effect stream (durability as data, `materialize!`\n   553\t  no longer returning an address synchronously) is deferred to a larger\n   554\t  write-path redesign.\n   555\t- **Garbage collection.** Content reachability and reclamation belong to a\n   556\t  higher-level retention policy; immutable content otherwise accumulates\n   557\t  forever.\n   558\t- **Async hydration.** Readers of remote or async B-tree content use the\n   559\t  hydration adapter (`dao.data.btree.storage/hydration-storage` and\n   560\t  `hydrate!`), but the async variants (`hydrate-async`, `store-tree-async`)\n   561\t  are deferred until an async DaoJing backend exists. See\n   562\t  `docs/design/dao.data.btree.md` §5.4.\n   563\t\n   564\t  The remote half of that deferral is the **stepped client**: a\n   565\t  non-blocking remote handle with the `request-put` / `request-get` /\n   566\t  `request-materialize` / `step` / `abandon` shape — a client the caller\n   567\t  steps on a host that cannot wait. It is owed to the async hydration\n   568\t  work (`docs/design/dao.data.btree.md` §5.4), not to the v1 deletion: it\n   569\t  needs multi-id dispatch and per-materialization records rather than the\n   570\t  blocking driver's per-id step, and it forces a consumer change on\n   571\t  B-tree hydration.\n   572\t\n   573\t  **Status 2026-09-18 (stepped client):** the client itself is built —\n   574\t  `dao.jing.remote.step` wraps one `dao.stream.rpc` client state with\n   575\t  per-materialization records and id routes, `step` advancing in the\n   576\t  recorded fixed order (retry unsent, poll, terminal-abandon, issue\n   577\t  verify reads in put-id order, drain-and-route), the `:present` verify\n   578\t  hop hashing the read-back against the address exactly as\n   579\t  `materialize!` does, one published completion per materialization\n   580\t  carrying its put id, and no payload retained or published anywhere.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
