{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_41","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^'\"\\\\(defn decode|defn-.*decode|re-encode|canonical|bytes= .*encode|encode v' src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc | tail -70; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc | sed -n '1655,1735p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc | sed -n '390,454p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '188,217p;407,423p;490,523p'; nl -ba test/dao/jing_test.cljc | sed -n '323,347p'; nl -ba test/dao/jing/mem_test.cljc | sed -n '230,279p'\"","aggregated_output":"2:  \"DaoJing's canonical CBOR value codec and portable numeric operations\n6:   `encode` turns one supported value into its canonical payload bytes;\n7:   `decode` accepts exactly one canonical payload and returns its value.\n23:   * sets: tag 258 over elements sorted by their canonical bytes;\n32:   decoded value to re-encode to the identical bytes. Boring's decoder is not\n38:   corpus refusal classes as a keyword (`:non-canonical`,\n712:   kind, decimal scale and zero sign; canonical NaNs are equal. Symmetric\n848:   sign counts, and every NaN is the one canonical NaN), within decimal the\n897:   width is not significant, and canonical NaNs are equal (every NaN has\n898:   the one canonical encoding). Collections recurse the way equiv does,\n1026:   collection, members at depth d, refusing duplicate canonical encodings\n1129:  \"The canonical CBOR payload bytes of one supported value (byte[] on the\n1152:  \"Order two supported values by their canonical encodings: the shorter\n1523:(defn decode\n1524:  \"The value of one canonical Jing payload (byte[], Uint8Array or\n1527:   profile, and any payload whose value does not re-encode to exactly\n1536:      (when-not (bytes= bs (encode value))\n1537:        (refuse :non-canonical))\n   390\t      (fn []\n   391\t        (when-not (:closed? @state)\n   392\t          (swap! state assoc :closed? true)\n   393\t          (close-file! f))\n   394\t        nil))))\n   395\t\n   396\t\n   397\t;; =============================================================================\n   398\t;; Constructor\n   399\t;; =============================================================================\n   400\t\n   401\t(defn create-content-file\n   402\t  \"Open or create a content file at path: validate every complete frame,\n   403\t   truncate any incomplete tail (F3), replay every record into an\n   404\t   in-memory map of address to payload bytes (F2, F4), and return the\n   405\t   plain-data byte-store handle {:put-bytes-fn :get-bytes-fn :close-fn}\n   406\t   (D1). A\n   407\t   frame that is not a canonical [digest payload] record, whose digest is\n   408\t   not its payload's under a registered algorithm, or whose payload is not\n   409\t   canonical fails the open, loudly and without mutation (F4).\"\n   410\t  [path]\n   411\t  (let [f (open-file! path)]\n   412\t    (try (let [recovered (replay-records (recover! f))\n   413\t               state (atom {:closed? false, :content recovered})\n   414\t               lock #?(:clj (Object.)\n   415\t                       :cljs (js-obj)\n   416\t                       :cljd (Object.))]\n   417\t           {:put-bytes-fn (make-put state f lock),\n   418\t            :get-bytes-fn (make-get state),\n   419\t            :close-fn (make-close state f lock)})\n   420\t         (catch #?(:clj Exception\n   421\t                   :cljs :default\n   422\t                   :cljd Object)\n   423\t                e\n   424\t           (try (close-file! f)\n   425\t                (catch #?(:clj Exception\n   426\t                          :cljs :default\n   427\t                          :cljd Object)\n   428\t                       _\n   429\t                  nil))\n   430\t           (throw e)))))\n   431\t\n   432\t\n   433\t;; =============================================================================\n   434\t;; The test's view of a path (F5)\n   435\t;; =============================================================================\n   436\t\n   437\t(defn records\n   438\t  \"The decoded, validated [address value] record vector of the file at\n   439\t   path -- every frame in order, equal duplicates included. Opens with the\n   440\t   same validate-then-truncate pass as create-content-file, reads and\n   441\t   closes. This is the file backend's test-facing view (D4): what the\n   442\t   in-memory map holds is observable through the handle; what the file\n   443\t   holds is observable here.\"\n   444\t  [path]\n   445\t  (let [f (open-file! path)]\n   446\t    (try (mapv (fn [[address payload]]\n   447\t                 [address (jing/segment-value address payload)])\n   448\t               (recover! f))\n   449\t         (finally (close-file! f)))))\n   188\t\n   189\tCanonicalization may understand representation-level structure such as maps,\n   190\tsets, numbers, strings, and byte arrays. It must not understand domain concepts\n   191\tsuch as datoms, index orders, manifests, or any notion of a root.\n   192\t\n   193\tThe encoding is the pinned, cross-platform canonical CBOR profile of\n   194\t[`dao.jing.cbor.md`](dao.jing.cbor.md), implemented by `dao.jing.cbor/encode`\n   195\tand `decode` and frozen by the `cbor-v1` fixture corpus:\n   196\t`dao.jing/canonical-bytes` is `dao.jing.cbor/encode`, and every address is\n   197\tthe selected registry digest of exactly those bytes on every host.\n   198\t\n   199\tThe contract, precisely: collection metadata (on maps, sets, vectors, lists,\n   200\tand seqs) and symbol metadata are address-significant, except\n   201\treader-position keys (`:line`, `:column`, `:end-line`, `:end-column`), which\n   202\tare stripped before hashing, and empty metadata, which is dropped rather than\n   203\ttreated as distinct from no metadata; a metadata map that itself carries\n   204\tmetadata is refused. Lists and seqs of equal content share one address;\n   205\tvectors, sets, and maps are each their own type and never collide with\n   206\tanother. Keywords and symbols are encoded from their namespace and name\n   207\tfields, so a symbol never collides with the number or string its text\n   208\tmimics. Byte arrays are addressed by content. Numeric kind, decimal scale,\n   209\tand float zero sign are address-significant; host integer width is not.\n   210\tAnything outside the supported domain (records, functions, host objects,\n   211\tcharacters, `#inst`, `#uuid`) is refused loudly rather than addressed.\n   212\t`materialize!`'s `:present` read-back is verified by hash and byte for byte\n   213\tagainst the proposed bytes, so a metadata-only mismatch is caught as a real\n   214\tcollision.\n   215\t\n   216\t## Multihash Content Addressing & Algorithm Registry\n   217\t\n   407\t  address already holding the same bytes reports `:present` and is never\n   408\t  overwritten; get answers a fresh copy, so no caller can change a stored\n   409\t  snapshot. `entries` (verified and decoded) and `entry-bytes` (raw copies)\n   410\t  are the test-facing views.\n   411\t- `dao.jing.file/create-content-file` -- a content-addressed byte store\n   412\t  backed by a private framed append-only file. Each log record is one\n   413\t  canonical CBOR array `[digest payload-bytes]` (the raw digest the address\n   414\t  carries, then the exact canonical payload bytes) behind a 4-byte\n   415\t  big-endian signed length prefix, written through a write lock,\n   416\t  acknowledged only after the log is flushed, and replayed on open to\n   417\t  rebuild the in-memory map of address to bytes. Replay parses every\n   418\t  complete frame with the shared codec and validates it -- a two-element\n   419\t  array of byte strings, a digest that is the payload's under a registered\n   420\t  algorithm (which then names the address), a canonical payload -- before\n   421\t  anything is mutated; only then is an incomplete tail truncated. A corrupt\n   422\t  complete frame, or a nonempty file with no valid first frame, fails the\n   423\t  open with the file untouched. The store guarantees idempotent close,\n   490\tdefault, :sha256 selectable); `segment-key` mints\n   491\t`:segment/<algorithm>-<digest>` addresses; `segment-address?` is the strict\n   492\taddress test the backend layer enforces; `segment-matches?` (over a value)\n   493\tand `segment-bytes-match?` (over canonical bytes) are the total\n   494\taddress-directed verification predicates. As recorded in *Canonical\n   495\tencoding*, the encoder is the pinned canonical CBOR profile.\n   496\t\n   497\t**Backends are byte stores.** A handle is `{:put-bytes-fn f, :get-bytes-fn\n   498\tg, :close-fn c}`: put takes an address and canonical payload bytes and\n   499\tanswers `:inserted` or `:present`; get takes an address and a caller-supplied\n   500\tnot-found sentinel and answers bytes or that sentinel. `materialize!` encodes\n   501\ta payload exactly once, derives the address from those bytes, and hands the\n   502\tsame bytes to the backend; on `:present` it reads the bytes back and\n   503\tverifies them by hash and byte for byte. `get` hash-verifies the backend's\n   504\tbytes and decodes them. `dao.jing.remote` and `dao.jing.dht` carry the bytes\n   505\tas padded standard-alphabet Base64 text inside their existing Transit and\n   506\tdatagram envelopes, and run the one ingress canonicality check (strict\n   507\tBase64, digest, canonical decode) before any received bytes are stored or\n   508\tserved.\n   509\t\n   510\t## Open items and current limitations\n   511\t\n   512\t- **Retired by the canonical CBOR codec (2026-09-24).** The transitional\n   513\t  order-normalized printer, its three residuals (symbol metadata not\n   514\t  address-significant, pathological print collisions, ambient print-var\n   515\t  leakage), byte arrays hashed by identity, and the \"backends must fail\n   516\t  closed on metadata they cannot carry\" item are closed:\n   517\t  `dao.jing/canonical-bytes` is `dao.jing.cbor/encode`, every backend is a\n   518\t  byte store that carries the canonical bytes verbatim, and the file\n   519\t  backend's frame is the CBOR `[digest payload-bytes]` record. The\n   520\t  `pr-str`/EDN round-trip validator the file backend once needed is gone.\n   521\t  What remains open from that family is below.\n   522\t- **ClojureDart's `list` mints metadata.** On ClojureDart, `(list ...)` and\n   523\t  `(apply list ...)` return a list carrying `cljd.core`'s own reader metadata\n   323\t        (is (= (jing/segment-key payload {:algorithm :sha256}) sha-addr))\n   324\t        (is (str/starts-with? (name sha-addr) \"sha256-\"))\n   325\t        (is (= payload (jing/get h sha-addr ::missing)))))))\n   326\t\n   327\t\n   328\t(deftest put-receives-the-derived-address-and-canonical-bytes\n   329\t  (testing \"the backend effect is invoked as (put-bytes-fn address bytes)\n   330\t            with the payload's canonical bytes, encoded once: the bytes\n   331\t            handed over are the very bytes the address digests\"\n   332\t    (let [seen (atom nil)\n   333\t          payload {:v 1}\n   334\t          h {:put-bytes-fn (fn [a bs] (reset! seen [a bs]) :inserted),\n   335\t             :get-bytes-fn (fn [_ _] nil)}]\n   336\t      (jing/materialize! h payload)\n   337\t      (let [[address bs] @seen]\n   338\t        (is (= (jing/segment-key payload) address))\n   339\t        (is (cbor/byte-payload? bs) \"the backend sees bytes, never a value\")\n   340\t        (is (cbor/bytes= (jing/canonical-bytes payload) bs))\n   341\t        (is (jing/segment-bytes-match? address bs)\n   342\t            \"the address is derived from exactly these bytes\")))))\n   343\t\n   344\t\n   345\t(deftest materialize-is-idempotent\n   346\t  (testing\n   347\t    \"duplicate materialization returns the same address, reports\n   230\t;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   231\t;; Mutation isolation: bytes are copied on the way in and on the way out\n   232\t;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   233\t\n   234\t(deftest a-caller-cannot-mutate-a-snapshot-through-its-input-array\n   235\t  (testing\n   236\t    \"changing the byte array handed to put after the put changes nothing\n   237\t            the store holds\"\n   238\t    (let [h (mem/create-content-mem)\n   239\t          payload {:isolated \"input\"}\n   240\t          address (jing/segment-key payload)\n   241\t          bs (bytes-of payload)]\n   242\t      (is (= :inserted ((:put-bytes-fn h) address bs)))\n   243\t      (set-byte! bs (dec (count (vec bs))) 0)\n   244\t      (is (= payload (jing/get h address ::missing))\n   245\t          \"the stored snapshot still decodes to the payload\")\n   246\t      (is (cbor/bytes= (bytes-of payload) ((:get-bytes-fn h) address ::missing))\n   247\t          \"the stored bytes are the original canonical bytes\"))))\n   248\t\n   249\t\n   250\t(deftest a-caller-cannot-mutate-a-snapshot-through-an-output-array\n   251\t  (testing\n   252\t    \"changing the byte array a get answered, or one the snapshot view\n   253\t            answered, changes nothing the store holds\"\n   254\t    (let [h (mem/create-content-mem)\n   255\t          payload {:isolated \"output\"}\n   256\t          address (jing/materialize! h payload)\n   257\t          out ((:get-bytes-fn h) address ::missing)\n   258\t          view (get (mem/entry-bytes h) address)]\n   259\t      (set-byte! out 0 0)\n   260\t      (set-byte! view 0 0)\n   261\t      (is (= payload (jing/get h address ::missing))\n   262\t          \"a later read still verifies and decodes\")\n   263\t      (is (cbor/bytes= (bytes-of payload) ((:get-bytes-fn h) address ::missing))\n   264\t          \"a later read answers the original bytes\")\n   265\t      (is (= {address payload} (mem/entries h))\n   266\t          \"the snapshot view is unaffected by mutation of an earlier view\"))))\n   267\t\n   268\t\n   269\t(deftest entries-verify-before-decoding\n   270\t  (testing\n   271\t    \"the test-facing entries view hash-verifies every snapshot against its\n   272\t            address, so seeded forged bytes surface as a refusal, never as\n   273\t            a value\"\n   274\t    (let [address (jing/segment-key {:claimed 1})\n   275\t          h (mem/create-content-mem {address (bytes-of {:forged true})})]\n   276\t      (is (throws? #(mem/entries h)))\n   277\t      (is (cbor/bytes= (bytes-of {:forged true})\n   278\t                       (get (mem/entry-bytes h) address))\n   279\t          \"the raw snapshot view still shows what was planted\"))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
