{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_44","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,190p' collab/1790245519256-reviewer-daojing-cbor-swap.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md; rg -n 'order-normalize|order-normalized-print|canonical-print|put-content-fn|get-content-fn' src test docs/design/dao.jing.md docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md | head -65; git diff --unified=0 | perl -ne 'if (/\"'^'\"\\\\+\\\\+\\\\+ b\\\\/(.*)/) {\"'$f=$1; $ln=0} elsif (/''^@@.*'\"\\\\+(\\\\d+)(?:,(\\\\d+))?/) {\"'$ln=$1-1} elsif (/''^'\"\\\\+(?\"'!'\"\\\\+)(.*)/) {\"'$ln++; $s=$1; $a=($s =~ /[''^'\"\\\\x00-\\\\x7f]/); \"'$l=length($s); if($a || $l>80){print \"$f:$ln \",($a?\"nonASCII \":\"\"),($l>80?\"len=$l\":\"\"),\"'\"\\\\n\\\"}} elsif (/\"'^ /) {$ln++}'\"' | head -45\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_44","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,190p' collab/1790245519256-reviewer-daojing-cbor-swap.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md; rg -n 'order-normalize|order-normalized-print|canonical-print|put-content-fn|get-content-fn' src test docs/design/dao.jing.md docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md | head -65; git diff --unified=0 | perl -ne 'if (/\"'^'\"\\\\+\\\\+\\\\+ b\\\\/(.*)/) {\"'$f=$1; $ln=0} elsif (/''^@@.*'\"\\\\+(\\\\d+)(?:,(\\\\d+))?/) {\"'$ln=$1-1} elsif (/''^'\"\\\\+(?\"'!'\"\\\\+)(.*)/) {\"'$ln++; $s=$1; $a=($s =~ /[''^'\"\\\\x00-\\\\x7f]/); \"'$l=length($s); if($a || $l>80){print \"$f:$ln \",($a?\"nonASCII \":\"\"),($l>80?\"len=$l\":\"\"),\"'\"\\\\n\\\"}} elsif (/\"'^ /) {$ln++}'\"' | head -45\"","aggregated_output":"I’m using the `code-review-pr` skill because this is a branch-level adversarial review. I’ll first load its review protocol and the four governing design documents, then inspect the branch diff and run only read-only checks/tests that do not modify repository state.\nThe governing contract is clear on the key failure modes: one CBOR encoder, strict ingress canonicality, byte-snapshot immutability, and nondestructive file replay before torn-tail truncation. I’m now establishing the exact branch delta and will trace those requirements through implementation and tests rather than relying on the task’s file list alone.\nThe core swap is indeed direct (`canonical-bytes` delegates only to `cbor/encode`), and memory/file now retain encoded byte snapshots. I’m checking the less obvious edges next: replay’s algorithm reconstruction, canonical-frame rejection before truncation, mutation isolation on returned binary values, and whether the updated tests actually exercise these paths.\nI found a concrete immutability breach: the memory handle now exposes its atom containing mutable host byte arrays, so callers can mutate stored addressed content behind the API. I’m doing a final static verification pass now, including the explicit ASCII/80-column gate and checking whether the tests cover the new byte helpers and replay format.\nThe focused JVM suite passes (80 tests, 3,200 assertions), and the changed implementation/test sources pass clj-kondo and cljstyle. Those checks do not cover the exposed-byte mutation path or enforce the requested ASCII/80-column limits; both remain review findings. I’m consolidating the final evidence now, including replay’s exact algorithm-selection behavior.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-24 10:33:59 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-24 17:33:59 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\nVerdict: request changes.\n\nP1 | [src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:104](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:104) | The public handle exposes `:state`, whose content now contains mutable host byte arrays. A reproduction mutated stored `[1 2]` into `[1 3]`; `mem/entries` returned the forged value while `jing/get` detected an integrity error. `entries` also decodes without address verification at line 118. | Keep the atom inaccessible, expose a closure-based snapshot view, and use `segment-value` for verified decoding. Add mutation-isolation tests for both input and exposed state/output.\n\nP1 | [src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:457](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:457) | The required shared byte-store boundary was not implemented. `segment-key` encodes once, then `materialize!` passes the original value to the backend; memory and file encode it again at [mem.cljc:38](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:38) and [file.cljc:344](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:344). Instrumentation confirmed two encoder calls for one insertion, contrary to the encode-once contract at [dao.jing.cbor.md:98](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:98). | Encode once in the Jing wrapper, derive the address from those bytes, and delegate through `:put-bytes-fn`/`:get-bytes-fn`; keep public APIs value-facing.\n\nP2 | [docs/design/dao.jing.md:190](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.md:190) | The authoritative design still declares the order-normalized printer current, lists it as an open item at line 486, and describes file records as `[address payload]` at line 407. This contradicts the implemented clean break. | Replace the transitional-printer sections with the canonical-CBOR and byte-store contracts, document `[digest payload-bytes]`, and retire the superseded open items.\n\nP2 | [test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc:222](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc:222) | The file fail-closed tests still inject EDN text at lines 229–235. They prove that arbitrary non-CBOR fails, but do not exercise valid-CBOR wrong shapes, bad digest lengths, digest mismatches, noncanonical payload bytes, or mutation isolation. | Construct raw CBOR frames for every replay refusal class and add binary/metadata/rich-number and byte-mutation round trips.\n\nP3 | [test/dao/data/psset_fixtures.cljc:1](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/data/psset_fixtures.cljc:1) | The strict hygiene gate fails: this generated file contains non-ASCII and 860 lines over 80 columns; representative long output starts at line 12. Other named files also contain non-ASCII or long lines, including [hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:94](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:94) and [digest-table.edn:104](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/resources/dao/jing/digest-table.edn:104). | Update the fixture generator to emit ASCII and wrap address literals, regenerate, and reflow the remaining named files.\n\nConfirmed correct:\n\n- `canonical-bytes` delegates directly to `cbor/encode`; the three printer functions are absent from production sources.\n- `segment-matches?` is total, while `segment-bytes` and `segment-value` perform the expected hash/codec validation.\n- File frames are canonical CBOR byte-string pairs. Replay infers the algorithm by testing the digest against each registered algorithm, reconstructs the address from the first match, rejects no-match frames, validates all complete frames, and only then truncates a torn tail: [file.cljc:116](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:116), [file.cljc:279](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:279).\n- No legacy decoder, printer alias, or old-address fallback was found.\n\nVerification: focused JVM suite passed 80 tests / 3,200 assertions; clj-kondo reported 0 errors and 0 warnings; cljstyle passed. No files were edited.\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:513:  order-normalized printer, its three residuals (symbol metadata not\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:128:It continues to return the UTF-8 bytes of the current order-normalized, metadata-aware print encoding.\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:505:The algorithm-registry epic lands first over the current order-normalized print encoder.\ntest/resources/dao/jing/digest-table.edn:32:;; order-normalized print encoder; regenerated 2026-09-24 along with every\ndocs/agents/delegate-invocation-reference.md:152 len=168\ndocs/agents/delegate-invocation-reference.md:153 len=168\ndocs/agents/delegate-invocation-reference.md:154 len=168\ndocs/agents/team.md:129 len=170\ndocs/agents/team.md:130 len=170\ndocs/agents/team.md:131 len=170\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:6706 nonASCII len=101\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:6710 len=115\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:6725 len=81\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:6739 len=88\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:6740 len=89\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:6741 len=89\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:6749 len=118\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:6750 len=127\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:6752 len=120\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:6758 nonASCII \nsrc/cljc/dao/data/btree/storage.cljc:3 nonASCII \nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1501 len=82\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/remote/step.cljc:314 len=88\ntest/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:294 len=82\ntest/dao/jing/remote_test.cljc:147 len=81\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
