          registered algorithm, or whose payload bytes are not one\n285:    (let [p (bytes-of \"ok\")\n289:          noncanonical (host-bytes [0x18 0x01])\n291:          truncated (host-bytes [0x82 0x01])\n293:          trailing (host-bytes [0x01 0x02])\n295:          indefinite (host-bytes (concat [0x9f]\n296:                                         (bytes->ints (cbor/encode d))\n297:                                         (bytes->ints (cbor/encode p))\n300:              [[\"not CBOR at all\" (jing-file/->bytes \"[[[not cbor\")]\n301:               [\"three-element array\" (cbor/encode [d p (host-bytes [1])])]\n302:               [\"text payload, not bytes\" (cbor/encode [d \"ok\"])]\n305:                (cbor-frame (host-bytes (take 16 (bytes->ints d))) p)]\n307:                (cbor-frame (digest-of (bytes-of \"different\")) p)]\n308:               [\"non-canonical payload bytes\"\n310:               [\"truncated payload bytes\"\n316:              frame-bytes (frame-of record)]\n317:          (append-raw-bytes! path frame-bytes)\n321:               (is (= (byte-count frame-bytes) (file-length path))\n326:(deftest a-mutated-byte-fails-the-open-without-mutation\n328:    \"a stored frame whose payload bytes change on disk no longer hashes to\n332:          payload {:mutation \"target\"}\n338:            original (last (bytes->ints\n371:      (append-raw-bytes! path (frame-of (jing-file/encode-record good \"ok\")))\n372:      (append-raw-bytes!\n373:        path (frame-of (cbor-frame (digest-of (bytes-of \"ok\"))\n374:                                   (bytes-of \"other\"))))\n385:  \"The F3 proof shape: one valid record, a torn tail of raw bytes, and the\n388:  [prefix torn-bytes]\n395:    (append-raw-bytes! path torn-bytes)\n419:            two payload bytes (hand-written, not the v1 fixture: the torn\n420:            bytes must follow a valid record)\"\n422:                        (host-bytes (into (int32-be 999) [1 2])))))\n427:    (torn-tail-recovers \"torn-subprefix\" (host-bytes [7 7]))))\n432:    (torn-tail-recovers \"torn-negative\" (host-bytes (int32-be -1)))))\n456:            ((:put-bytes-fn handle) address (bytes-of payload))))\n475:                                                    ((:put-bytes-fn handle)\n477:                                                     (bytes-of payload)))))))]\n   490\t          h1           (jing-file/create-content-file path)\n   491\t          p1           {:msg \"first payload\", :n 1}\n   492\t          p2           {:msg \"second payload\", :n 2}\n   493\t          addr-b3      (jing/materialize! h1 p1)\n   494\t          addr-s256    (jing/materialize! h1 p2 {:algorithm :sha256})\n   495\t          addr-p1-s256 (jing/materialize! h1 p1 {:algorithm :sha256})]\n   496\t      (is (= :blake3 (jing/segment-algorithm addr-b3)))\n   497\t      (is (= :sha256 (jing/segment-algorithm addr-s256)))\n   498\t      (is (= :sha256 (jing/segment-algorithm addr-p1-s256)))\n   499\t      (is (not= addr-b3 addr-p1-s256))\n   500\t      (is (= p1 (jing/get h1 addr-b3 ::absent)))\n   501\t      (is (= p2 (jing/get h1 addr-s256 ::absent)))\n   502\t      (is (= p1 (jing/get h1 addr-p1-s256 ::absent)))\n   503\t      (jing/close! h1)\n   504\t      (let [h2 (jing-file/create-content-file path)]\n   505\t        (try\n   506\t          (is (= p1 (jing/get h2 addr-b3 ::absent)))\n   507\t          (is (= p2 (jing/get h2 addr-s256 ::absent)))\n   508\t          (is (= p1 (jing/get h2 addr-p1-s256 ::absent)))\n   509\t          (is (= 3 (count (jing-file/records path))))\n   510\t          (finally\n   511\t            (jing/close! h2)\n   512\t            (cleanup-file path)))))))\n   120\t### `canonical-bytes`\n   121\t\n   122\tThe signature remains:\n   123\t\n   124\t```clojure\n   125\t(canonical-bytes value)\n   126\t```\n   127\t\n   128\tIt continues to return the UTF-8 bytes of the current order-normalized, metadata-aware print encoding.\n   129\t\n   130\tNo algorithm-specific canonicalization is permitted. BLAKE3 and SHA-256 receive identical bytes for the same value.\n   131\t\n   132\tThe future CBOR epic will replace this implementation outright. It will not add a second coexisting encoder or alter this function's public role.\n   133\t\n  1523\t(defn decode\n  1524\t  \"The value of one canonical Jing payload (byte[], Uint8Array or\n  1525\t   Uint8List).\n  1526\t   Refuses malformed CBOR, trailing data, anything outside the closed\n  1527\t   profile, and any payload whose value does not re-encode to exactly\n  1528\t   these bytes. Returned byte strings are fresh copies.\"\n  1529\t  [bs]\n  1530\t  (when (zero? (blen bs))\n  1531\t    (refuse :malformed-cbor \"empty input\"))\n  1532\t  (let [[item pos] (read-item bs 0 1)]\n  1533\t    (when (not= pos (blen bs))\n  1534\t      (refuse :trailing-data (str (- (blen bs) pos) \" bytes after the item\")))\n  1535\t    (let [value (item->value bs item)]\n  1536\t      (when-not (bytes= bs (encode value))\n  1537\t        (refuse :non-canonical))\n  1538\t      value)))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_50","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-24 15:55:32 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-24 22:55:32 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\nThe five prior findings are now:\n\n1. **CLOSED** — The memory handle exposes closures rather than `:state`, returns byte copies, and verifies `entries` before decoding ([mem.cljc:96](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:96), [mem.cljc:132](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:132), [mem.cljc:145](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:145)). A separate concurrent-mutation issue remains below.\n2. **CLOSED** — `materialize!` encodes the payload once, hashes those bytes, and passes them to `:put-bytes-fn` ([jing.cljc:512](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:512)).\n3. **CLOSED** — `dao.jing.md` now specifies CBOR, byte stores, and `[digest payload-bytes]` frames ([dao.jing.md:193](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.md:193), [dao.jing.md:411](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.md:411)). The separate hash-registry design remains stale.\n4. **PARTIALLY CLOSED** — Replay tests now cover the requested CBOR refusal classes and unchanged-file rejection ([file_test.cljc:278](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc:278)). Memory tests cover caller-array isolation ([mem_test.cljc:234](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing/mem_test.cljc:234)); the file tests cover disk-byte corruption, but not caller input/output array mutation ([file_test.cljc:326](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc:326)).\n5. **CLOSED for the three previously named fixtures** — The current `psset_fixtures.cljc`, `hash_registry_contract_test.cljc`, and `digest-table.edn` contain only ASCII and lines of at most 80 columns. The wider changed-file hygiene gate still fails.\n\nActionable findings:\n\nP1 | [src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1176](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1176) | The Node run’s durable round-trip errors when `:yin.debruijn/value` contains decoded `#dao.jing/float64 1.5`; the canonical classifier accepts numbers but not this carrier ([debruijn.cljc:273](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:273), [test-cljs.log:311](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/target/test-cljs.log:311)). Its second failure compares a decoded carrier to bare `2.5` ([pipeline_test.cljc:585](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc:585), [test-cljs.log:316](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/target/test-cljs.log:316)). The codec *requires* a JS carrier on decode ([dao.jing.cbor.md:375](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:375)); these are unresolved consumer/test defects, not an accepted passing limitation. | Make the VM’s canonical-value and numeric paths carrier-aware, compare portable content in the test, and require a green Node suite.\n\nP2 | [src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:51](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:51) | Both byte-store puts verify a caller-owned mutable array *before* copying it ([file.cljc:348](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:348)). A concurrent caller mutation between validation and copy can store or append bytes that no longer hash to the address; duplicate checks also compare the original array ([mem.cljc:60](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:60), [file.cljc:360](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:360)). | Copy first, then validate, frame, and compare only the snapshot.\n\nP2 | [src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1536](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1536) | `decode` re-encodes every value. Consequently ordinary accepted-snapshot reads through `segment-value` re-encode too ([jing.cljc:470](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:470)), contrary to the explicit read-path contract ([dao.jing.cbor.md